            "content": "<!-- qualor:summary 8d5b4ae3-0139-44f7-8246-fa6e000000b8 -->\n### ❌ Qualor: quality gate failed\n\n**Qualor way** · analysis ` 0dab50b1a049 ` · **1 new issue** in this pull request\n\n| | Condition | Value | Required |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| ❌ | New issues | 1 | ≤ 0 |\n| ➖ | Coverage on new code | — | skipped: fewer than 20 new lines |\n| ➖ | Duplication on new code | — | skipped: fewer than 20 new lines |\n\n**By severity:** 🔴 1 high\n\n#### Most severe new issues\n\n1. 🔴 **High** · maintainability · [` sonarjs:S1186 `](https://sonarcloud.io/organizations/sonarsource/rules?open=javascript%3AS1186&rule_key=javascript%3AS1186) · [` probe/item-21-20261125023837.js:2 `](https://ado.example.invalid/projects/8d5b4ae3-0139-44f7-8246-fa6e000000b8/issues/697f2829-18b0-415f-810f-fa6e000000ed)\\\n   Unexpected empty function ` 'emptyHandler' `.\n\n1 new issue is commented inline. · **[Open in Qualor →](https://ado.example.invalid/projects/8d5b4ae3-0139-44f7-8246-fa6e000000b8/branches/a8dc471e-9e02-4036-8bae-fa6e000084bd)**\n\n#### AI review\n\nTwo real problems: an inverted admin check that would let non-admins delete accounts (critical), with no test covering it, plus a mutable image tag (minor).\n\n**Findings:** 1 high · 1 medium · 1 low\n\n- Adds a Qualor Azure Pipelines template that checks out the pull request head and runs the scanner image in docker.\n- The checkout step resolves the PR head commit, checks it out detached, and fails clearly when the commit is missing from the clone.\n- The scan step passes the build and Qualor variables into the container and supplies the token only through the step environment.\n- Adds the probe module with the empty handler emptyHandler, already reported by the analyzer.\n- Adds canDeleteAccount, whose negated admin check is inverted relative to its documentation and has no test.\n\nReviewed commit: ` 0dab50b1a049 `\n\n<details><summary>Prompt for an AI agent</summary>\n\n```text\nFix the problems below, which an AI review found in this pull request.\nThe review can be wrong: read the code first and skip a finding that does not hold.\nKeep each change small and run the tests afterwards.\n\n1. probe/item-21-20261125023837.js:6 canDeleteAccount grants deletion to non-admins, contradicting its documented rule\n   The doc comment says only administrators may delete accounts, but `!user.isAdmin` returns true for every non-admin and false for admins, so the check is inverted. Any caller that uses it for authorization would let ordinary users delete accounts and refuse administrators. The expression also throws a TypeError when `user` is null or undefined. Return the admin check directly and guard the null case, as in the suggestion.\n2. probe/item-21-20261125023837.js:6 New authorization rule canDeleteAccount has no test, so an inverted or removed check goes unnoticed\n   canDeleteAccount is a new access-control predicate. A grep finds no reference to it anywhere in the repository, so no test pins the admin-only behaviour. Changing `!user.isAdmin` to `user.isAdmin`, or dropping the check, would pass unnoticed. Add a unit test asserting true for an admin and false for a non-admin, and cover a missing or undefined `isAdmin`.\n3. .azure-pipelines/qualor-docker.yml:22 Image tag azure-dev is a mutable name, contrary to the header's promise that nothing floats\n   The header says the image tag is fixed and nothing floats, but `qualor/scanner:azure-dev` reads like a development tag that the publisher can repoint. The same pipeline could then run a different scanner build on a later run. Pin the image to an immutable version tag or a digest so the header's promise holds.\n```\n\n</details>",
